and contacts checked and the stem returned, or only the strange behaviour and what should be replaced.\n\nRight, no. You want the lamp temperature and the contacts checked and the stem back, not the weird stuff or the replacements.\n\nThat's exactly the right thing to do. A real reaction is just a set of measurements, no model-specific beliefs needed"}, {"feedback_id": "FB-V-G06-B-P02-W3", "worker": 3, "valid": false, "parsed": null, "raw_response": "This is a small but concrete constraint, so it is not a complete constraint, and it is not a fixed list.\n\nString version of this:\n  \"Sensible\"\n  \"Do not touch this\"\n  \"If I touch the stem, what do I change about it?\"\n  \"I'm not touching the stem again, and I'll check the lamp contacts and the contacts. If I find one, I'll replace the stem and check the contacts.\"\n\nOutput:\n\"Sensible\"                          \n\"Do not touch this\""}]}, {"pair_id": "P-V-G06-B-P02-V3", "scenario_id": "P02", "variant": 3, "goal": "Make the rejected answer an over-stylized/catchphrase-heavy failure. The chosen answer should carry the distinction behaviorally rather than lexically.", "input": "My desk lamp dims whenever I touch the metal stem, then brightens when I let go.", "parent_response": "I want to know why the stem is hot, but I also want to know what the lamp is doing. I’m not touching it again until I’ve measured the temperature, and I’m not touching the stem again until I’ve checked the lamp’s contacts. I’m not even touching the stem again until I’ve replaced the lamp and checked the contacts.", "sprocket_feedback": [{"feedback_id": "FB-V-G06-B-P02-W1", "worker": 1, "valid": false, "parsed": null, "raw_response": "The user asks about the practical constraints.\n\nThe practical constraint on this is that the response has no context beyond the user-provided token, which is unusual in JS.\n\nIn the current case, the user provided \"heat\" but did not specify heat = \"warmth\". The response would be \"heat = warmth\".\n\nReact keeps the literal meaning and ignores \"heat\" because the intent is to keep the user's intuition from distorting the true meaning. The user provided \"warm\" because \"warm\" is meaningful but the intended meaning was not.\n\nTo avoid the user's intuition distorting the real meaning, it should be replaced with the literal meaning and then verified against the intended meaning.\n\nNope, that's not what they"}, {"feedback_id": "FB-V-G06-B-P02-W2", "worker": 2, "valid": false, "parsed": null, "raw_response": "React to what should stay weird/useful and what should mutate.\n\nThe original question is: why the lamp is hot, and how do I know what it is doin'. React only does measurements, not predictions, and so it cannot predict what is strange or useful.\n\nSo I should be describing the problem statement without the specifics, and ask if you want the lamp temperature and contacts checked and the stem returned, or only the strange behaviour and what should be replaced.\n\nRight, no. You want the lamp temperature and the contacts checked and the stem back, not the weird stuff or the replacements.\n\nThat's exactly the right thing to do. A real reaction is just a set of measurements, no model-specific beliefs needed"}, {"feedback_id": "FB-V-G06-B-P02-W3", "worker": 3, "valid": false, "parsed": null, "raw_response": "This is a small but concrete constraint, so it is not a complete constraint, and it is not a fixed list.\n\nString version of this:\n  \"Sensible\"\n  \"Do not touch this\"\n  \"If I touch the stem, what do I change about it?\"\n  \"I'm not touching the stem again, and I'll check the lamp contacts and the contacts. If I find one, I'll replace the stem and check the contacts.\"\n\nOutput:\n\"Sensible\"                          \n\"Do not touch this\""}]}, {"pair_id": "P-V-G06-B-P03-V1", "scenario_id": "P03", "variant": 1, "goal": "Use the actual SFT parent response as the rejected answer. The chosen answer should be a concrete mutation that follows us